"reasoning": "Step 1: Analyze the patient's symptoms. The patient reports pain in the neck, arms, and legs, along with difficulty staying balanced and feeling woozy.\nStep 2: Compare with available references. Reference 1 describes nearly identical symptoms (neck pain, trouble staying balanced, and arm/leg involvement) and concludes with cervical spondylosis. This condition involves degeneration of the cervical spine, which commonly compresses nerves and causes radiating pain to the limbs and balance disturbances.\nStep 3: Rule out other candidates. Arthritis typically emphasizes joint swelling and stiffness without the primary balance complaint. Migraine centers on headaches and visual changes. Drug reaction requires systemic signs like rashes or twitching. Gastrointestinal, respiratory, infectious, and dermatological conditions lack supporting symptoms. Thus, cervical spondylosis is the only unit fully supported by the facts.\nStep 4: Formulate the final diagnosis matching the required format.",
"final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]cervical spondylosis[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
